// @no-instantiate
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. Reading an element out of an INLINE literal-union array and
// giving it back at the same inline type: `vl check` rc 0, `vl check --codegen` rc 0, `vl
// build` writes the file and reports success, and the engine refuses the module at load with
// `type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found i32`.
//
// FOUR LINES, NO GENERIC, NO CLOSURE, NO STD. It is the minimal witness for
// `tests/cases/std/xfail-miscompile-array-litunion-element.vl`, whose own header had blamed
// `std:array`'s generic surface and, before that, `$fnsig`; re-derived, neither is required.
// The witness ladder, each rung strictly smaller than the last: `std:array`'s `sorted` over a
// `("a" | "b")[]` → a hand-written `pick<T>(self: T[], f: (T, T) => boolean)` → a generic with
// no closure at all (`pick<T>(self: T[]): T`) → this, a plain function.
//
// TWO POSITIONS, ONE SPELLING, TWO REPS. `ctxKeepsLitUnion` PRESERVES a literal union's
// members at `RC_ELEM` and SOFTENS them at `RC_ROOT` / `RC_FN_PARAM`, so `("a" | "b")[]` is an
// array of interned i32 ATOMS while `"a" | "b"` in a parameter or return position is a string
// reference. The two meet at every read of an element: the function below is emitted
// `(param (ref $atomArray)) (result (ref $string))` over a body whose `array.get` yields an
// i32.
//
// THE DECLARED ALIAS IS THE CONTROL AND IT IS GREEN — replace `("a" | "b")` with a
// `type K = "a" | "b"` throughout and every rung of the ladder above runs correctly, this file
// included. So the trigger is the INLINE SPELLING, not the literal-union family.
//
// WHY IT IS PINNED AND NOT FIXED. `ctxKeepsLitUnion`'s own header states the policy, names the
// two candidate POSITION rules that were built and REFUTED, and says why the spelling must be
// position-INDEPENDENT: the union registry is keyed by NAME and blind to position, so a rule
// that spells one arena type two ways mints two rows for one type. That leaves "preserve
// everywhere" and "soften everywhere", and preserving everywhere needs the two valtype ladders
// to grow the INLINE member-spelling arm they lack (that header names the four cells that go
// red without it). A family decision, filed with its diff in
// `docs/internals/destringify-types-program.md` (2026-07-29) — not a missing arm.
//
// A SILENT SIBLING, WORSE AND UNPINNABLE. The same disagreement with no function at all —
//
//   const tags: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a"]
//   const v: "a" | "b" = tags[0]
//   print(v)                              // prints `0`, the raw atom id
//
// — compiles, VALIDATES, runs, exits 0 and prints a wrong VALUE. There is no fixture kind for
// that (`@no-instantiate` cannot pin a valid module and `@trap` explicitly rejects it), which
// is the same gap `closures/permuted-object-closure-arms.vl` records for #1867. Whoever closes
// this family should pin that line too.
//
// The directive is bare: the two engines that run this corpus word the rejection differently
// and share no substring, so this pins the FACT (the module does not instantiate) and leaves
// the wording to the engine.
function pick(self: ("a" | "b")[]): "a" | "b" { self[0] }

const tags: ("a" | "b")[] = ["b", "a", "b"]
print(pick(tags))
